const title = 'Gongfu Brewing at Home: The Setup You Actually Need';
const description = 'You don\'t need a $200 tea table to brew gongfu style at home. The honest starter setup — what to buy, what to skip, and the habits that matter more than gear.';
---

<BaseLayout
  {title}
  {description}
  articleType="BlogPosting"
  author="Chinese Tea Guide"
  datePublished="2026-08-10"
  dateModified="2026-08-12"
  ogImage="/images/chinese-tea-brewing.avif"
>

  <article class="section-container py-12 max-w-[760px]">
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-warm-900 mb-6">{title}</h1>
    <p class="text-xs text-zinc-400 mb-6">Reading time: 6 minutes</p>

    <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">Ask the internet how to start brewing gongfu style and you'll be sent down a rabbit hole of $200 tea tables, yixing clay collections, and tools with Chinese names you'll forget the moment you close the tab. It's all lovely stuff. None of it is required.</p>

    <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">Gongfu cha — literally "tea made with skill" — is a method, not a budget. The whole point is a high leaf-to-water ratio and many short infusions, each revealing a new layer of the tea. Here's the setup that actually gets you there.</p>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">The three things you need</h2>
    <div class="space-y-4 mb-8">
      <div class="card p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-1">1. A gaiwan ($8-20)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">A bowl, a lid, a saucer — that's it. The porcelain is neutral, so it won't absorb or add flavors, which means one gaiwan handles every tea you own. The lid does double duty as a strainer: tilt it just enough to let the liquor through and hold back the leaves. Start here. If you own one thing for brewing, make it a 100-150ml gaiwan.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-1">2. A fairness pitcher ($10-30)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">After each steep, pour the gaiwan into the pitcher first, then from the pitcher into cups. This equalizes strength — the first pour from a gaiwan is always weak and the last is over-steeped. Without the pitcher, nobody at the table gets the same cup twice. Glass lets you watch the liquor color, which is half the fun.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-warm-900 mb-1">3. A couple of small cups ($5-25)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed">Gongfu servings are 30-60ml for a reason: you're tasting concentrated infusions, and you'll drink 6-15 of them. Any small porcelain cup works. Add a tall aroma cup later if you fall in love with the ritual of inhaling the empty cup.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">The upgrade that matters more than any teaware</h2>
    <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">A variable-temperature kettle. Green tea wants 175°F, oolongs 195-205°F, pu-erh 212°F. Boiling water destroys delicate greens; lukewarm water leaves oolongs flat. A kettle that remembers a few temperatures costs about as much as a decorative tea tray and changes every single brew you make. If you have $50 to spend, spend it here before anywhere else.</p>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">What you can skip (for now)</h2>
    <div class="card p-5 mb-8">
      <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed list-disc pl-5">
        <li><strong class="text-warm-900">A Yixing teapot.</strong> Wonderful, but its superpower — absorbing tea oils into unglazed clay over years — means one pot is permanently dedicated to one tea type. Buy it after you know what you drink every day.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-warm-900">A tea boat/tray.</strong> A plate or a small cutting board catches overflow. That's the entire job.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-warm-900">Tea pets and fancy scoops.</strong> Charming. Non-essential.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-warm-900 mt-10 mb-4">The three habits that matter more than gear</h2>
    <div class="space-y-4 mb-8">
      <div class="card p-5"><p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-warm-900">Use enough leaf.</strong> Western brewing uses about 1-2g per 200ml. Gongfu uses 5g per 100ml — roughly a third of the vessel filled with dry leaf. Scared? Start at 3-4g and adjust. Undershooting is the most common beginner mistake by far.</p></div>
      <div class="card p-5"><p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-warm-900">Rinse before you brew.</strong> Pour hot water over the leaves and discard it immediately. It wakes the leaves, warms the vessel, and (for compressed teas) loosens the surface. Ten seconds, every session.</p></div>
      <div class="card p-5"><p class="text-sm text-muted leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-warm-900">Watch the leaves, not the clock.</strong> Times are starting points. Tightly rolled oolongs need a few extra seconds to open up; well-rested leaves re-steep longer. The first steeps are short (10-45s), and every round after adds a little time. Let the color in the pitcher be your guide.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="card p-6 bg-leaf-50 border-leaf-200 mb-8">
      <p class="text-sm text-accent leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-leaf-900">The honest bottom line:</strong> a gaiwan, a pitcher, two cups, and a kettle that holds a temperature. Under $100 total, and you can brew everything on this site with it — from a delicate Silver Needle to a 15-steep shou pu-erh. The ceremony is a means to a better cup, not the other way around.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="text-warm-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">Ready to try it? Start with an oolong like <a href="/teas/tie-guan-yin" class="text-accent underline">Tie Guan Yin</a> — it's built for the method — or a <a href="/teas/pu-erh-shou" class="text-accent underline">shou pu-erh</a> that forgives every timing mistake you'll make in week one. Both reward you across 5+ infusions, which is exactly what gongfu is for.</p>
  </article>

